{
  "image_name": "0adef5ae3f93fa06b2f26a563dc139f7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how mouse trophoblast stem cells (mTSCs) move from self-renewal toward trophoblast differentiation, and how the underlying transcription-factor network shifts during this process, with a comparison to human cells.\n\nAt the top, mTSCs are shown giving rise to differentiated trophoblast types, including spongiotrophoblast, trophoblast giant cells, glycogen trophoblast, and syncytiotrophoblast. The central schematic proposes a regulatory circuit that changes across this transition. In the self-renewing state, factors such as TEAD4/YAP1, SOX2, ESRRB, KDM1A, TFAP2C, ELF5, EOMES, and CDX2 appear interconnected, maintaining the stem-cell program and binding regulatory DNA elements. As differentiation begins, the network seems to reconfigure: SOX2 and ESRRB become less central, while factors including HAND1, GATA3, ETS2, ELF5, and TFAP2C are emphasized in directing differentiated trophoblast gene expression.\n\nThe lower panel condenses these dynamics as timelines. In mouse, SOX2, ESRRB, CDX2, and EOMES are strongest in the TSC niche and decline with differentiation, whereas TFAP2C, GATA3, ETS2, and especially HAND1 persist or rise later. ELF5 shows an intermediate pattern. In human trophoblast, the program differs somewhat: CDX2 appears more limited, while TEAD4, ELF5, TP63, TFAP2C, and GATA3 are highlighted more broadly.",
